From 8c7563cd42a4e50bcb3fcc6880f1cf56ba86be3f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 10:12:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 29/31] Fix the stats charts

Three separate problems, all visible at once, none of them the user's data.

The ranking was being thrown away. Both charts selected their top N in a
subquery that ordered correctly, then wrapped it in WHERE id IN (...) -
and SQLite does not preserve a subquery's order through IN. The right six
songs came back in essentially arbitrary order, so the number one track
could appear anywhere in the list. Joining against the ranked set instead
keeps the order.

The artist chart measured something else entirely. It read the playCount
table, which stores one total per calendar month, so its window was
rounded out to whole months - a "1 month" period actually covered five
and a half weeks, "1 year" covered twelve and a half months - and it
ranked by number of plays while the song chart ranked by time listened.
It also counted only songs marked as in the library, which on a real
collection discarded about a quarter of all plays: an artist could be
absent from the chart while their track sat at number one directly above
it. It now reads event, the same source the song chart uses, so the two
agree on window, measure and scope.

Also moves the player background to Appearance, where people look for it,
and says plainly what the minimum playback duration actually controls -
it decides what reaches your history and your stats, which the old label
gave no hint of.
